🤖 Agent: That’s a meaningful project — and there are several good avenues to pursue. Below I’ve laid out a practical, step‑by‑step research plan, key record types to look for, relevant institutions/collections, privacy/processing notes, and how I can help if you want me to draft letters or search queries.

Quick checklist to gather first
- Full name (including any alternate spellings and maiden names)  
- Exact or approximate date and place of birth (town/village, county, region)  
- Parents’ names (and their birthplaces if known)  
- Approximate year(s) of emigration and port of arrival (if you know)  
- Any U.S. locations they lived in, naturalization info, or employers  
Having these makes searches much faster and more accurate.

Step‑by‑step research plan
1. Talk with family first  
   - Ask relatives for any documents, letters, photos, passenger tickets, naturalization papers, older passports, or even anecdotal details (ship name, year, sponsors). Even a vague year or port helps narrow searches.

2. Search U.S. arrival/immigration records (online databases)  
   - Passenger arrival manifests (NY, Boston, other ports) and border-crossing lists. These often show last residence, age, occupation, traveling companions, and ship name.  
   - Useful databases: Ancestry, FamilySearch, and the National Archives’ passenger manifest collections. (If you want, I can suggest specific search terms/formats.)

3. Look for naturalization / immigration case files in the U.S.  
   - USCIS Genealogy Program (historic “A‑files” and naturalization files) — you can request immigrant/naturalization files (there’s a genealogy request process/FOIA route). These files commonly contain place of birth, date of birth, arrival ship, and affidavits.  
   - National Archives (NARA) also holds some naturalization records depending on where the person naturalized.

4. Check U.S. federal and state records  
   - Census records (1940, 1950, etc.) show residence, immigration year, naturalization status.  
   - Social Security application (Form SS‑5) lists birth place and parents’ names — obtainable via request (or found indexed in genealogy sites).  
   - City directories and local newspapers (arrival notices, wedding announcements, obituaries).

5. Investigate Displaced Persons / Refugee records (post‑WWII context)  
   - Many WWII refugees were processed through DP camps and international agencies (UNRRA, IRO). Records were kept and many are now at archives.  
   - The Arolsen Archives (formerly International Tracing Service) in Germany holds extensive DP and refugee records from WWII/postwar — searchable and they will do searches for relatives.  
   - U.S. records relating to the Displaced Persons Act and visa files may be at NARA or State Dept archives.

6. German records and local sources (for pre‑migration life)  
   - Civil registration (Standesamt) holds birth, marriage, death records for German towns. Older church (Kirchenbuch) records are also important. Note privacy/closure rules vary (some records restricted for a number of years).  
   - Town archives (Stadtarchiv) or regional archives may have wartime and local records. If you can identify the town, I can suggest what archive to contact and sample wording.

7. Holocaust / wartime databases (if applicable)  
   - If there’s any possibility your great‑aunt’s family was affected by wartime events, check Yad Vashem Central Database, US Holocaust Memorial Museum databases, and Arolsen Archives. Even where not, these resources can contain migration/transport info.

8. Use DNA and family trees as a complement  
   - AncestryDNA, 23andMe, and FamilyTreeDNA can connect you to relatives and often provide clues about regional origins. Matches sometimes point to cousins who have documents or family histories.

Where to request records and what they typically yield
- USCIS Genealogy Program — naturalization/A‑files (arrival details, petitions, affidavits).  
- NARA — passenger lists, immigration records, visa case files in some series.  
- Arolsen Archives — DP camp and displaced persons records (names, camp registrations, transport lists).  
- Local German Standesamt / church archives — birth/baptism records, family registers.  
- US Social Security Administration (SS‑5) — birthplace and parents’ names (copy of application).  
- Local libraries/newspaper archives — arrival, marriage, obituary notices, community news.

Privacy, timing, and costs
- Some records require proof of relationship or have privacy restrictions and may take time to process.  
- Archival searches may have fees (Arolsen, some municipal archives, USCIS genealogy fees, or paid “lookups” on commercial sites).  
- Expect some searches to take weeks; NGOs/archives often respond slower.

Sample first actions you can do right away
- Write down all family info you already know and photograph any documents.  
- Do an initial search on FamilySearch and Ancestry for passenger lists and census entries (I can suggest search terms).  
- If you want, give me your great‑aunt’s full name and any dates/places you know and I can propose targeted searches and sample queries/messages to archives.

